# Security Policy — dDRM, Sessions, CEK Containment

> **Authoritative companion docs** (read before changing anything in this area):
> - [`docs/core/DDRM_DECRYPT_RUNTIME.md`](../../docs/core/DDRM_DECRYPT_RUNTIME.md) — architecture, retry chain, file map.
> - [`docs/core/DDRM_SESSION_ARCHITECTURE.md`](../../docs/core/DDRM_SESSION_ARCHITECTURE.md) — server-owned P-256 session model (predecessor).
> - [`docs/core/CHIPOTLE_V3_PROTOCOL.md`](../../docs/core/CHIPOTLE_V3_PROTOCOL.md) — Lit Action protocol that decrypt depends on.
> - `pc2-node/data/lit-actions/universal-decrypt-chipotle.js` — the deployed TEE verifier. Pin its CID in `chipotle-client.ts`.

All rules below are **hard requirements**. Reviewer (human or AI) MUST reject any change that violates them. If a rule appears to block legitimate work, that means the design has a flaw — escalate, do not weaken the rule.

---

## 1. CEK containment invariants

The Content Encryption Key (CEK) is the most sensitive piece of material in the system. Two backends exist (`'js'` and `'wasm'`); the WASM backend is the default and has the strictest containment.

### 1.1 — No new CEK exit points

**MUST NOT** introduce any new code that:
- Returns a CEK (or its base64 / hex representation) from a function whose public type is anything other than `string` on `BackendSessionView._cekBase64` (the documented private field for the JS backend).
- Reads `view.cekBase64` outside the two legacy sites that still depend on it (`storage.ts renderViaWASM` JS-backend branch, `media.ts recoverMediaCEK` JS-backend branch). New consumers MUST call `view.decryptAsset` / `view.decryptSegment` / `view.decryptChunk` instead.
- Exposes the WASM L2 CEK across the FFI boundary. The `ddrm-decrypt` C ABI deliberately has **no `get_cek` export**. Adding one is a hard NO.
- Logs the CEK, includes it in HTTP responses, embeds it in error messages, stores it in any persistent store other than `cekSessionCache` (JS backend) for ≤ 5 minutes.

### 1.2 — Locality table (use this to audit any new code)

| Place | JS backend | WASM backend |
|---|---|---|
| After `unwrapEnvelope` | `BackendSessionView._cekBase64` (V8 string) — private | `ddrm-decrypt::REQUESTS[h].cek: Zeroizing<Vec<u8>>` |
| Cache | `cekSessionCache` (5 min TTL, LRU 1000 entries) | `wasmRequestCache` — caches the **handle only**, never the CEK itself |
| Playback | `MediaSession.cekBase64` (V8 string) | `MediaSession.wasmRequestHandle` — handle only |
| Renderer (`ddrm-renderer` WASM) | `RendererCommand.cek_b64` (passed via MemFS to the renderer) | **Never** — must use `mode: 'render_only'` with plaintext input |
| `cenc-decrypt` WASM (legacy CENC path) | `command.cek_b64` (passed via MemFS) | **Never** — must use `ddrm-decrypt::media::decrypt_segment` |

If a change adds a location not in this table, that change MUST be rejected or the table updated with the same review rigor as the original CEK refactor.

### 1.3 — `Zeroizing` invariant inside `ddrm-decrypt`

CEKs in the WASM crate live inside `RequestState.cek: Zeroizing<Vec<u8>>`. The `Zeroize` drop scrubs memory. **MUST NOT** copy CEK bytes out of that field into any non-zeroizing container (a plain `Vec<u8>`, a `String`, a serde-serialized field, etc.). The `with_request_cek` closure in `media.rs` is the only legitimate access pattern — borrow `&[u8]`, hand it to a cipher primitive, return only ciphertext or plaintext.

### 1.4 — Sealed sessionView interface

`ICencDecryptor` is the only sanctioned way for new consumers to use a session's decrypt capability. Method signatures **MUST NOT** be widened to accept or return CEK material. Any new method on `ICencDecryptor` must take plaintext / ciphertext + IV / key-id arguments and return plaintext / ciphertext — never the raw key.

